; Tutorial 1.10: Data Statements
;
; Data lines store fixed values right inside the program, such as level layouts, enemy waves or the notes of a
; tune. Read takes the next value, and Restore says where reading starts. This is a simple way to ship a game
; without any extra files.

Graphics 800, 600, 0, 2 ; A bigger window, so that everything printed fits.

; Read takes values in order, from the first Data line onwards. Each Read moves on by one value.
; Read needs variables to read into, so they are declared first.
Local itemCount, i, name$, price
Restore shopItems
Read itemCount
Print "The shop sells " + itemCount + " items:"
For i = 1 To itemCount
	Read name$, price
	Print "  " + LSet$(name$, 12) + RSet$(price, 4) + " gold"
Next

; A level map stored as text, one row per Data value. "#" is a wall, "." is floor, "@" is the player start and "$"
; is treasure.
Local mapWidth, mapHeight, x, y, row$
Local startX, startY, treasure
Restore levelMap
Read mapWidth, mapHeight
Dim tiles$(mapWidth - 1, mapHeight - 1)
For y = 0 To mapHeight - 1
	Read row$
	For x = 0 To mapWidth - 1
		tiles$(x, y) = Mid$(row$, x + 1, 1)
		If tiles$(x, y) = "@" Then startX = x : startY = y
		If tiles$(x, y) = "$" Then treasure = treasure + 1
	Next
Next

Print
Local rowText$
For y = 0 To mapHeight - 1
	rowText$ = ""
	For x = 0 To mapWidth - 1
		rowText$ = rowText$ + tiles$(x, y)
	Next
	Print "  " + rowText$
Next
Print "The player starts at " + startX + ", " + startY + " and there are " + treasure + " treasures to find."

; Restore can jump back to any label as often as you want, so the same Data can be read again.
Restore shopItems
Local firstItem$
Read itemCount
Read firstItem$
Print
Print "Reading the shop again from the start, the first item is the " + firstItem$ + "."

Print
Print "Press any key to quit."
WaitKey
End

; Data lines can go anywhere in the program. A label in front of them lets Restore find them.
.shopItems
Data 4
Data "Sword", 120
Data "Shield", 80
Data "Potion", 15
Data "Map", 5

.levelMap
Data 12, 6
Data "############"
Data "#@...#.....#"
Data "#.##.#.###.#"
Data "#..#...#$..#"
Data "#$.#####..$#"
Data "############"
